{
  "$comment": "Engine sound profiles, keyed by the `sound` id of engine modules (PartDef.modules[].sound). A profile is one layered voice per (vessel, profile) cluster: n engines of one profile sound as ONE source whose level is the power sum. Level in dB re the layers' normalised -20 dBFS: L = level.levelDb + efficiencyDb + 10 log10(refThrust / level.refThrust) + 10 throttleExponent log10(thrust per engine / refThrust) - throttleRangeDb (1 - lever) + 10 log10(n running), where lever = (throttleActual - minThrottle) / (1 - minThrottle) is where the engine sits in its throttle range (the player's lever by the sim's law; 1 for solids and fixed burns): an engine that can only throttle down to 50 % thrust still sounds clearly throttled. Layers add gainDb to L. core = recorded loop (rate and low-pass follow the lever), rumble = brown noise low-passed (deeper for bigger clusters: cutoff * n^-clusterExponent), hiss = pink noise band, crackle = shock-wave impulse train whose rate follows the lever and whose level is scaled by (nozzle ambient pressure / referencePressure)^pressureExponent, so it does not exist in vacuum. body = structure-borne coupling (heavily low-passed; level also falls with distance along the structure, mix.json propagation.structure). RCS modules (no sound field) use rcsModuleProfile. A cluster's voice plays only while the cluster sounds (running, an ignition/shutdown transient, or within voiceHold seconds of either). standbyVoices: up to this many silent clusters of the active vessel that can still fire (engines with ignitions left and not burnt out, RCS that is switched on) have their voice built ahead, one per frame, its sources not started: the nodes of a voice cost about a millisecond of main thread (0.44 ms of it the delay line), which then never falls in the frame of an ignition; a voice built ahead starts exactly as one built at the ignition (same nodes, same seeded offsets, same start time), and when its cluster has fallen silent and drained it is disposed and built again a frame later. 0 builds every voice when it first sounds.",
